{
  "The base of every control in the package. It gives each one the same `text`, `enabled`, `visible` and `tabStop`, and the same focus methods, whether the control is written in JSX and bound to state or created with `new` and changed programmatically.": "",
  "Every component of the package ([Button](/button), …) extends it; extend it for your own controls.": "",
  "Both ways are complete; the developer using the control decides.": "",
  "`Badge` is the control written in [Writing a control](#own).": "",
  "How a property gets its value": "",
  "A prop is a value or a function. A function of a reactive object (`enabled={() => !state.busy}`) keeps the property in step with it; this is how MotifJS keeps a prop live.": "",
  "Your data is used as you give it: the control never makes it reactive, never copies or changes it. Over plain data the function is read again when you call `reState()` on the control or on anything it sits in; you choose which kind of data to give.": "",
  "With `x-model` the model always shows the control's state: a change by the user, by your code (`ctrl.value = …`, `select()`, `show()`) or by a rule you gave (`decimals`, a mask, `min` / `max`) is written back to it.": "",
  "A property set programmatically takes the same: a function (`ctrl.enabled = () => !state.busy`) is bound in place of what was there and followed; a value (`ctrl.enabled = false`) drops what was bound, and from then on the code owns it.": "",
  "Every property is reactive to read: `{() => ctrl.text}` in JSX or a `bindings.watch` follows it, whichever way it changed.": "",
  "`x-wait` and `x-display` stay one-way: they show and hide from state, and nothing is written back. If the state must change when the control goes away, do it in `onDisposed`.": "",
  "Enabled": "",
  "Disabling a control disables everything inside it — other controls and plain elements alike, as a disabled `<fieldset>` does for form fields — and enabling it puts every element back exactly as it was. An element that was disabled on its own stays disabled; content added while the control is disabled is disabled too; a change the app makes in the meantime (a bound `disabled`) is what gets restored. Disabled containers nest: an element stays disabled while any of them, or its own value, says so.": "",
  "`button`, `input`, `select`, `textarea`, `fieldset`, `optgroup`, `option` get `disabled`: the browser blocks them and announces the state.": "",
  "Links, `[tabindex]`, `contenteditable`, `summary`, `iframe` get `tabindex=\"-1\"` and `aria-disabled=\"true\"`.": "",
  "`pointerdown`, `mousedown`, `click`, `dblclick`, `auxclick` and `contextmenu` are swallowed inside the disabled control, before any handler.": "",
  "Gets `data-disabled` for styling.": "",
  "A `<dialog>` window opened inside a disabled control is not disabled: owned windows stay usable.": "",
  "If focus was inside, it moves to the next usable element in the same window (or the one before it) instead of falling to `<body>`.": "",
  "Visible and TabStop": "",
  "`visible` uses core `hide()` / `show()`: a hidden control leaves the page and comes back in place, with its state. If focus was inside, it moves on first. `tabStop = false` only skips the control on Tab.": "",
  "Focus": "",
  "Focus never falls to `<body>` because of a control: disabling, hiding, removing or disposing the focused part moves focus to the next usable element. Focus rings show only for keyboard use (`:focus-visible`). Tab order, access keys and shortcuts: [Keyboard and focus](/keyboard).": "",
  "A control that remembers which control inside it is active and is a scope for Tab; the package's windows, tab pages and document views will be ones. The outermost container of a page or window also runs the `tabIndex` order (see [Keyboard and focus](/keyboard)).": "",
  "The control inside that has (or last had) focus, as `document.activeElement` is for the page; a nested container counts as one. Setting it focuses that control.": "",
  "Focus back to where it was the last time the container was active, else its active control, else the first in Tab order.": "",
  "protected getter": "",
  "`true` keeps Tab inside the container. `false` by default; a modal window returns `true`.": "",
  "Writing a control": "",
  "Extend `Control`, set the root element, and render `text` (and your own properties) with `observeProperty`: no effect is created, the callback runs on every change.": "",
  "The root element. `div` unless the subclass sets another (`button` for a button).": "",
  "Getter: the element that takes focus; the root by default. Composite controls override it. It runs from the constructor, so derive it from `this.element`.": "",
  "Renders a property without an effect: `apply` runs now and on every change, from a getter prop or programmatically.": "",
  "The storage behind the shared properties, for your own: tracked read, and set-from-code (which replaces a bound getter).": "",
  "Binds one of your own props: a getter is read now and followed by a binding of the control (so `reState()` reads it again), a value is stored once. Call it from your constructor.": "",
  "Render your own properties without an effect: override `applyProperty` (call `super`); read the current value untracked with `peekProperty`. Can run from the constructor: touch only `this.element`.": "",
  "Declares the access key parsed from `&Text` (`parseAccessKey`): written to `aria-keyshortcuts` as `Alt+K`, which is how Alt+K finds the control.": "",
  "Performance": "",
  "The base adds no listeners per control and pays only for what is used.": "",
  "Cost": "",
  "Nothing: no storage, no DOM write, no binding.": "",
  "Stored and written to the DOM once.": "",
  "One binding for that property, stopped when the control is disposed or another value or function is set.": "",
  "A small change counter, created the first time someone reads it.": "",
  "A direct DOM write; readers are notified through the counter.": "",
  "One pass over the control's subtree, plus one shared observer while anything is disabled (it disappears when nothing is).": "",
  "The control's text. Where it shows is up to the control: a button's caption, a form's title.": "",
  "`false` disables the control and everything inside it. See \"Enabled\".": "",
  "The HTML-style inverse of `enabled`, for habit: `disabled={() => state.busy}`. Ignored when `enabled` is given.": "",
  "`false` hides the control with core `hide()`; `true` shows it again with `show()`.": "",
  "`false` takes the control out of the Tab order; a click or `focus()` still focuses it.": "",
  "Order among the controls of the same container: Tab visits them in ascending order, ties in document order. Leave it unset where you can: document order is also the reading order of screen readers. See Keyboard and focus.": "",
  "Set programmatically with a value or a function, like the prop (see \"How a property gets its value\").": "",
  "This control's own value, as set or bound (like the HTML `disabled` attribute).": "",
  "Whether it can be used now: enabled itself and in every control it sits in (like CSS `:enabled`). Windows shown inside do not inherit it.": "",
  "This control's own value; a hidden parent does not change it.": "",
  "Position in the Tab order of its container.": "",
  "Writes `tabindex=\"-1\"` on the focus element while `false`, and puts back the element's own `tabindex` afterwards.": "",
  "The nearest ancestor that is a `Control`, not crossing a window.": "",
  "Enabled, on the page and focusable right now.": "",
  "Its focus element has focus. A DOM query, not reactive.": "",
  "Focus is on it or anything inside it. A DOM query, not reactive.": "",
  "Focuses the control; `false` when it cannot take focus (`canFocus`).": "",
  "What its access key (`&Save` → Alt+S, Control+Option+S on Apple platforms) does: focus; a button also clicks. Override it in your control.": "",
  "A shortcut active while focus is inside this control, removed when it is disposed. See Keyboard and focus.": "",
  "Not yet": "",
  "Arrow keys inside composite controls (one Tab stop, arrows inside): with the first composite control.": ""
}
